      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tularosa was named by Spanish conquistadors for the rose‑colored reeds that line the Rio Tularosa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trytularosa.org/local-history.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s original 49 blocks and 182 buildings were declared a historic district in 1979 and listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tularosa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1863 formal town plan mapped 49 blocks and recorded water rights, laying the groundwork for Tularosa’s enduring adobe architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tularosa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Rio Tularosa still runs along the north side of town, providing the water that keeps the town’s cottonwood shade trees alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tularosa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, the El Paso and Southwestern Railroad brought a line from Dawson through Tucumcari to Tularosa, connecting the town to regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tularosa%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Tularosa’s historic Mission Church, St Francis de Paula, was built after a promise that no one would die in the nearby Battle of Round Mountain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
